],[1601,"Signed into Authelia with the link still failing puts the fault behind Authelia: the\napp path is gated by the same policy as the rest of the coder name."],[934,"Six files seed into every new workspace, on first boot and only while the file is\nabsent:"],[1219,"So an alias edited or deleted in `litellm/config.yaml` moves in the Agents model\ndropdowns exactly as it does in the CLI."],[740,"So resolve the root from the environment first, then prove it, and fail loudly when the\nproof fails. `resolve_checkout` in `scripts/lib/common.sh` is that resolver and every CLI that\nreads a file the checkout alone holds calls it, so do not write a second one. It takes the calling\nscript's own directory as its last candidate and the marker to probe for (`docker-compose.yml` by\ndefault); the call site in every one of those CLIs is this:"],[241,"So the clone must exist at the same path inside the sidecar too, or it dies with\n`bind source path does not exist`."],[1167,"So the limitation tracks the model, not the runtime."],[928,"So the two compactions fail for unrelated reasons, and the fix for one is not the fix\nfor the other:"],[1638,"So the workspace build fails at **plan** time, before anything is created, and it\nprints in the workspace's build log rather than anywhere inside the workspace."],[1227,"So you can flip `coder` from the local Arc to a Spark box, or add a new alias, and no\nworkspace setting has to change."],[414,"Spark agent model group"],[1172,"Spreading across every reachable box is the point of running three of them, five full\n262144-token streams each. Pinning only narrows the candidate list first."],[1033,"Stable stack - `hermes` comes up with the rest:"],[1521,"Stable stack (`prod-stack` addresses the `evo-t1-prod` project by default; name\n`--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` to read a stack brought up by hand under the\ncheckout folder's own project name):"],[919,"Stack `up` starts that seeder in the background and keeps its output in\n`${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/coder-agents-seeder.log` (`CODER_AGENTS_SEED_LOG` moves it, and the\n`note:` line stack `up` prints names the path it chose). Read that file first when\nthe Agents chat is dead after a boot: the seeder's most common outcome is a `skipped:`\nline that exits 0 - no coder container yet, no db container, nobody registered - and\nan unwritable log path degrades to the old discard with a `warning:` instead of\nfailing the boot."],[1436,"Stack bring-up"],[1491,"Stack bring-up: bootstrap, ports, containers that refuse to start"],[878,"Stack ownership of the file rests on either marker spelling: the line-1\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment or the top-level `__evoT1Stack = true` key.\nBoth twins emit both, because the comment alone does not last - `grok mcp add`\npersists its own settings by rewriting the file and deletes every comment line, so a\ncomment-only marker handed the config to the user with no human in the loop, and the\ndefault lanes silently stopped arriving. A hand-edited config is left alone only\nwhile it carries one of them, so takeover means deleting **both** lines; delete the\nfile and restart to hand it back. When the guard does re-render, the CLI-owned tables\n`[cli]`, `[ui]` and `[marketplace]` are carried over rather than wiped, because the\nstack owns no approval mode - `[ui] permission_mode` / yolo stays a per-user call."],[481,"Stack-host bootstrap"],[486,"Stack-host bootstrap: the one script run straight from a shell"],[1039,"Start here"],[799,"Start the stable stack, and pull the models `OLLAMA_MODELS` names (a large download):"],[1244,"Starting a new repository with the quality gate already in place, in one command"],[1496,"Starting from the error text you actually saw and landing on one command"],[58,"Startup script templatefile rules"],[625,"Stopping the late-gate discovery of a coupling rule the graph already recorded"],[998,"Switch it inside the container with `hermes config set model.model chat` (or\n`agent-medium` for a thinking level), which edits the live config under `/opt/data`."],[462,"Switching either way is a **rebuild**, not a stop/start, because the nested mount\nis decided when the workspace is built.\ngrok-build-and-agents.md covers what the shared\nprofile holds and what seeds on first boot; the `workspace` pass above never\nreseeds `~/.grok/config.toml` for exactly that reason."],[1435,"Symptom-first list of what breaks on this box: the error string, the cause, the recipe, and what the fix costs."],[1650,"Symptom: `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` prints `== Preflight ==` and\n`== Variables ==`, then aborts inside `== Templates ==` with\n`push-template.sh: line 740: REPO_ROOT: unbound variable` and never prints a\n`summary:` line. Nothing was pushed."],[1640,"Symptom: `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` queues, then dies during\n\"Initializing Terraform Directory\". `workspace` cannot list versions for\n`coder/coder` and `kreuzwerker/docker` on `registry.terraform.io`;\n`devcontainer` cannot fetch `module \"devcontainers_cli\"` from\n`registry.coder.com`. Both lookups fail with\n`dial tcp: lookup … on 127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving`. Coder itself\nstays up; `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs coder` repeats the same Terraform\nlines."],[1644,"Symptom: `coder templates push` plans, then dies during \"Cleaning Up\" with\n`internal provisionerserver error: execute transaction: insert resource: insert\nagent app: upsert app: pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`.\nCoder logs `coder_app's id was unexpectedly empty` and then that Postgres error\nat `inserting template import job resource`."],[1647,"Symptom: both templates fail during \"Detecting persistent resources\" with\n`Terraform initialized in an empty directory!` / `Error: No configuration files`.\nCoder logs `unpacking source archive  size_bytes=30720` (tens of KB, not the\n~120 KB of real `.tf` / `.tftpl` contents)."],[421,"Telling a ported env key apart from a value you set yourself and must keep"],[1247,"Telling a refused GitHub permission apart from a broken render"],[1377,"Telling a router problem apart from an Authelia problem when a name fails"],[624,"Telling a rule class from a target name without opening a BUILD file"],[544,"Telling the contributor container, the Coder workspace and the stack host apart in one look"],[145,"Template source travels as a tar on stdin (`coder templates push --directory -`), so\nnothing is copied into the container or its volumes."],[40,"Template variables"],[191,"Template variables are pushed defaults; the create form can still override them per\nworkspace."],[364,"Template-side facts only; the long prose lives in\ndocs/grok-build-and-agents.md."],[1330,"Templates"],[1134,"TensorFold on the Spark boxes refuses an over-admitted stream with the Zig error value\n`NoRoom`, delivered as an HTTP 500 body. The OpenAI SDK hands that to LiteLLM with no\nint `status_code`, which at the pinned `v1.102.1` puts it on the status-less branch of\n`exception_mapping_utils`, so the router sees `APIConnectionError`."],[65,"Terminal and editor defaults"],[811,"Terminal and editor defaults"],[75,"Terminal and editor defaults inside the container, and workspace GitHub credentials"],[828,"Terminal and editor defaults, and the profile the templates seed"],[1465,"Terraform and the host `coder` CLI"],[680,"Testing with Bazel (the Bats harness)"],[1327,"Tests"],[571,"That `notes.md` is `notes_materialize` from the same library: it restores the canonical copy\nunder `~/.grok/notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/` over a live file that is empty or still the\nseeded stub, and copies a real live file back there, so a stub never walks over written\nnotes. Here that runs once, at seed time - the background watcher that repeats the back-copy\nabout every 30 seconds is a workspace-startup thing (`NOTES_SYNC_INTERVAL`; see\n../grok-build-and-agents.md) - so re-run the\nseeder to push a fresh edit to the canonical copy in this container."],[1209,"That 30-request burst was multi-session, fan-out-style traffic."],[593,"That container is the *laptop and CI* environment: it mounts the host docker socket and\nassumes a workstation daemon, neither of which belongs inside a workspace's nested dev\ncontainer."],[417,"That env comments always refresh from the twin, even when values stay"],[252,"That exposes the dev container as a sub-agent with its own terminal and apps, and the\nin-browser editor attaches to *that* sub-agent, so the editor runs inside the dev\ncontainer where the cloned repo's toolchain lives."],[371,"That folder is what code-server, the Web Terminal button and the Grok Build session\nopen on. Inside a dev container, code-server sees that same folder under its mount\ntarget, `/workspaces/<repo-name>`."],[171,"That form addresses the stack through `docker compose` instead, so it names the\nproject with `-p`. From the checkout root the folder name would have said the same thing."],[313,"That is also why `config.toml` cannot be per-workspace in that mode: it is one file, and\nthe last workspace to boot re-renders it for all of them, so this workspace's default\nmodel (or, in `devcontainer`, its model alias) and config extras overwrite whatever the\nprevious boot wrote - for every sibling."],[235,"That is the *AI workspace + DinD* shape, always on, plus a repository that brings its own\ntoolchain."],[584,"That is the host ingress: it `docker run`s `evo-t1-dev:latest` as this process's uid:gid\n(`$(id -u):$(id -g)`), with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER=$(id -un)` so Bazel has a writable home\nand a name for a uid the image passwd file does not list, and runs the same suites CI\nruns. A macOS laptop gets the Linux result without a Linux box. Build the tag first with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image` if the daemon does not have it."],[135,"That is the server's own plain listener (`CODER_HTTP_ADDRESS` binds `0.0.0.0:3000`\ninside the container), reached without crossing the reverse proxy, Authelia, or the WAN."],[971,"That is the whole point of the service: stack ops by conversation. \"Recreate the dev\nstack and tell me which container restarts\", \"why is the coder agent offline\", \"run the\ngate and fix what it reports\" - each one runs with a plan first and an approval card\nbefore the mutating command."],[886,"That is what makes \"survives workspace recreate\" true rather than a hope: an edit is on the\nvolume within roughly one interval, so a recreate no longer waits on someone restarting the\nworkspace to pick up what was typed since the last boot. What is not promised is the tail"],[326,"That is why the `.git` suffix is stripped with `sed` rather than the shorter\n`${leaf%.git}`: a braced expansion would add an interpolation `main.tf` does not pass."],[457,"That pipes a PAT straight into that container's `gh` credential store - no `.env`\nedit, no template push - and `gh auth logout` inside the container, or a rebuild,\nremoves it. The repositories a token can reach are whatever that GitHub app\ninstallation was granted."],[1299,"That preset/CLI pair is the whole hand-off. It is where this repo hands a new project over, and the\nonly coupling between the two repositories: this repo names the scaffold it clones, the scaffold\nnever names this one."],[1636,"That probe only asks whether the daemon answers `docker version`. It says nothing\nabout the workspace that used to reach it."],[1192,"That request routes elsewhere, to whichever box `least-busy` picks."],[662,"That target resolves to 198 source files through 25 declarations: the gap is the `filegroup` and\n`scripts/lib` expansion under each label. `labels(\"data\", ...)` is the narrower question (\"what does\nthe BUILD file say\"), `files` is the wider one (\"what is in the sandbox\"), and the two disagreeing is\nusually the bug."],[649,"That warning is the honest version of \"nothing depends on this\", and it is the reason to keep\nstderr with a query. Go to the gate that owns the map instead of guessing its label:"],[1296,"The **`New project (scaffold ready)` preset** in both templates is a form shortcut. It pins the\ngolden image, turns on the DinD sidecar in the workspace template, and passes this stack as\n`additional_repos`, which is what puts `scripts/new-repo.sh` inside a fresh workspace beside the\nproject being started. The scaffold does not ride along: the CLI fetches it from GitHub when it\nruns. It is a preset, so it only sets parameter values."],[1571,"The **agent bar** keeps `:4000` off its own path: the workspace page's `litellm` tile\nis `coder_metadata` text (`value = var.litellm_url`), a read-out rather than a\n`coder_app`, so `litellm-gmktecbeast.` is never consulted here. Health-check the\nproxy with `curl http://host.docker.internal:4000/health/liveliness` on the stack\nhost. The app lane under `A workspace app link answers Bad Gateway` below is the\n**VS Code Web** editor link and stays on 13337 inside the workspace."],[977,"The **Chat tab** is the primary UX. It embeds the Hermes TUI - a PTY over WebSocket\non `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` - so the terminal agent and the browser are the same\nprocess, and approval prompts render as cards in the chat rather than vanishing into\na detached tty. Hermes sends a keepalive every 20 seconds. Traefik and Authelia have\nto pass the `Upgrade` hop. A close code **4403** is the Host / Origin / peer guard.\nCompose sets `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` from `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the seed file\ndoes not carry `public_url`). The seeded `dashboard.trusted_proxies` is\n`[\"172.16.0.0/12\"]`, which is what makes Hermes honour `X-Forwarded-Proto` from the\nrouter (the Secure cookie and `wss`). `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` is environment\nand applies when the container is recreated. An existing `hermes-data` volume does\nnot pick up `trusted_proxies` until a reseed or `hermes config set` - the volume\nseed is copy-once. The reseed command is below."],[1570,"The **model lane**: a process inside the workspace dials\n`http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1` directly. `host.docker.internal` is the docker\nbridge gateway (`172.17.0.1`), compose publishes the proxy's container port 4000 on\nhost port 4000, and nothing between the two authenticates. `LITELLM_BASE_URL` and the\ncoding CLIs use this lane."],[1590,"The **VS Code Web** button is the one app this stack still proxies: its URL is\n`/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/code-server` on the coder hostname, because\n`coder_app.code_server` sets `subdomain = false`. So a failing app link is an app-lane\nfailure, and a 200 from `litellm-gmktecbeast.` is not evidence about it."],[261,"The `--` ends Bazel's own flags, so a URL that begins with a dash still reaches the\nscript. Every fence below is one complete command."],[676,"The `//` half is the honest measure of what the gates can see, and it moves as files are added, so\ntreat any absolute total in a document as a snapshot rather than a fact to assert."],[707,"the `# @function` block above the definition, with Globals/Arguments/Outputs/Returns."],[742,"The `${REPO_ROOT:-}` / `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}` default is not decoration: these scripts run\nunder `set -euo pipefail`, and a bare `${REPO_ROOT?}` aborts with `REPO_ROOT: parameter not\nset` the moment a sandbox or CI runner leaves the variable unset."],[1755,"The `0.0.0.0` bind refusal above is a different failure: empty basic-auth env,\nnot this public path. Full notes: hermes.md."],[1533,"The `agent` -> `coder` fallback keeps chat answering while a Spark is down, which is\nexactly when tools disappear."],[1160,"The `agent` lane deliberately carries the `openai/` provider prefix. `ollama_chat/`\nwould route those boxes through the Ollama-specific code path, and `api_base` has to\ninclude `/v1`."],[471,"The `agent` model group is three deployments behind one LiteLLM alias, one per\nSpark box, and these are the keys that fill it:"],[1153,"The `agent` row is ONE model group with three deployments, one per Spark box,\nall equal."],[615,"The `bazel-map` CLI, its verbs, and the `--json` split that feeds `bazelisk test`"],[20,"The `ci` workflow (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs for `push` to `main` and for\n`pull_request` targeting `main`. The other shipped workflow, `deploy-docs`, also runs\non a `push` to any branch (`branches: [\"**\"]`) whose diff touches its `paths:` list -\n`docs/**`, `docs-site/**`, the two `scripts/ci/` helpers it publishes through\n(`publish-pages-tree.sh`, `docs-alias.sh`), and its own workflow file - and by\n`workflow_dispatch`."],[927,"The `compaction` pin above belongs to Coder's chat agent and to nothing else. It is one\nof the four auxiliary lanes the seeder writes into Coder's own model-config store, so it\ndecides which alias serves *that* agent's summariser and title call. It never reaches a\nGrok Build CLI: the CLI reads no Coder table, and its compaction summary is an\n**in-session call on the session model** - the `[models] default` alias, `agent` here -\nwhich is why the summary inherits that lane's output ceiling (measured on the wire at\n`max_tokens: 32768`) instead of a cheap helper lane's. `[models] session_summary` is not\nthat call either; the key serves titles and the session recap."],[900,"The `context7` server (library and API docs) also renders by default, and is the\nonly cached one. `context7_mcp` (default true) is its gate, and the block it\nrenders runs `mcp-cache-proxy` in front of the official `context7-mcp` - two packages\npinned in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image, so parking\nor restoring it is a flag plus a template push, while changing the packages\nthemselves takes an image rebuild. The proxy keeps its SQLite cache under\n`~/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/`, which rides the owner's shared\nprofile volume in `shared` mode, and the cache TTL is 12 h: the first lookup of a\nlibrary reaches the Context7 API and needs network, while an identical lookup inside\nthe TTL is answered locally. `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is optional and the lane does not\ndepend on it: an empty value sends no `Authorization` header at all, so the server\nhandshakes and answers at the public rate limit, with private libraries the only\nthing out of reach. A key in `.env` plus a re-push fills the env. What *parks* the\nlane on purpose is `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` in `.env` (relayed by `push-template.sh`) or\n`--var context7_mcp=false` on a template push; a `[mcp_servers.context7]` block\nmissing from a render that got neither of those is a defect, not a park. The lookup\norder is fixed - `resolve-library-id` first, then `query-docs` on the id it returns"],[355,"The `devcontainer` editor cannot set `agent_id` with\n`local.use_dc ? coder_devcontainer.repo[0].subagent_id : coder_agent.main.id`.\n`coder_devcontainer.repo` is also gated on `start_count`, so the stop /\ntemplate-import ephemeral plan has an empty tuple even with the toggle on\n(the default), and that ternary then fails with `Invalid index` / `empty\ntuple`. `local.code_server_agent_id` is\n`concat(coder_devcontainer.repo[*].subagent_id, [coder_agent.main.id])[0]`:\nthe sub-agent when that resource exists, the parent agent on the stopped\npage. The start-gated `coder_script.code_server` uses the same local."],[352,"The `devcontainer` fork has no persistent prefix to reuse - both toggle modes rebuild\nthe container that hosts the editor - so it installs the pinned release fresh under\n`/tmp/code-server` on every start, and bakes the settings seed as base64 into `settings_b64` (empty when the\ntoggle is off, so the shell skips the merge). The DinD wait lives in\n`templates/devcontainer/dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`."],[43,"The `devcontainer` template"],[737,"The `docker` stub passes `exec`/`compose exec` through by default (`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RUN=1`),\nso a binary the script execs inside a container lands on its own stub; set\n`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RUN=0` with `STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RC`/`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_OUT` for the\n\"the daemon answered, and it said No\" case."],[1510,"The `first boot? run bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap to create it` hint alongside it\nmeans the checkout has no `.env` at all: that is the bootstrap path. `DASHBOARD_ENV_FILE`\npoints the rotation at another file and exists for test harnesses; there is deliberately\nno flag for it, because a flag that names a file this CLI overwrites is one mistype away."],[392,"The `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` pair - reached through the helper, because\n`gh auth git-credential` echoes either name whenever one is set. A helper that does not\nclear the pair therefore answers with the injected copy and never asks the store: that\nis the shadow, and it is why a hand-edited helper or a hand-rolled askpass script is the\nwrong instrument (see\nthe push-denied entry)."],[899,"The `github` server (`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`) renders by default even\nthough it needs a token and outbound network: with GitHub tools on, the agent can\ndrive the repository it is working in, and the cheaper failure is the block that\nrenders with no credential and pays one failed handshake per session. `github_mcp`\n(default true) is the gate; `$GH_TOKEN` comes from Coder External Auth, never from a\nPAT."],[207,"The `grok_default_model` aliases are `agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`,\n`agent-xhigh`, `coder`, `coder-fast` and `chat`, plus a **Custom** entry when the\nvariable names another one."],[672,"The `kind` filter is what makes an `rdeps` usable. A bare `rdeps(//..., \"litellm/config.yaml\")`\nincludes `//:test-fast`, which reaches every file and therefore tells you nothing about this one:"],[1242,"The `New project` workspace preset and the optional Coder Task"],[1519,"The `ollama` service is IPEX-LLM on the Arc 140T, not Ollama from a registry."],[895,"The `searxng` MCP stdio server is what answers instead, and it is the search lane\nrather than a fallback: both templates render that block gated on `search_mcp`,\nwhich defaults to true. It dials `$SEARXNG_URL` (default\n`http://host.docker.internal:8181`), the same key `main.tf` puts in the agent\nenvironment. It carries nine tools - `search`, `image_search`, `news_search`,\n`video_search`, `music_search`, `paper_search`, `fetch_content`, `autocomplete`,\n`list_engines` - and an agent's two workhorses are the first and the seventh:\n`search` for anything that is not a library question, `fetch_content` for a URL\nalready in hand. Measure the self-hosted lane with:"],[1157,"The `spark-*` rows are a different thing: the same Qwen2.5-Coder and Qwen2.5\nweights, served over Ollama on the Spark boxes instead of on the Arc."],[27,"The `templates/common/` payloads"],[841,"The `tmux` profile gives each terminal of a folder its own pane. The base session\nname is unchanged: `vscode-<folder>-<hash>`. A new terminal walks into one of\nthat folder's sessions only while no client is attached to it, oldest first -\nthat is the closed-tab rejoin, so a tab you closed or reloaded leaves its\nsession unattached and the next terminal finds it with its history intact, and\n`ssh` + `tmux attach -t <session>` lands there too. A terminal opened when\nevery session of the folder already has a client takes the next numbered\nsibling instead: `vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`, `-3`, ... Sessions persist\nunattached until the container restarts, so a reattached terminal still\nreaches the output a closed tab produced. `tmux ls` lists the names and\n`tmux list-clients` says who is looking at each. Every target the helper\ngives tmux carries the `=` exact-match prefix (a bare name is answered by any\nsession starting with it, so a closed `foo` could be answered by a live\n`foo-2` and re-create the mirror), and a reopen whose session vanished\nmid-attach raises a fresh session rather than exiting along with the tab.\nTwo tmux behaviors shape that path: a session raised by a tab's own\nforeground `new-session` has its first pane closed when that client exits\n(superuser#575909),\nwhich is why the helper creates detached and attaches separately; and with\nthe default `exit-empty` the server itself leaves when its last session does\n(tmux#2081), which is why a\nreopen probes the server before raising anything - a tab whose server is gone\nhas ended normally, and re-raising there would boot a server for a terminal\nnobody is looking at (tmux has no server probe: `has-session` answers the same\nfor a missing session and a missing server, which is why upstream declined to\nadd `check-server`, tmux#2523).\nNo upstream issue accounts for the session loss: `destroy-unattached` is off,\nso a detached session is meant to survive, and tmux gives a session no grace\nperiod after it loses its last client\n(tmux#5821 is an open request for\none)."],[351,"The `workspace` fork installs code-server **only when the pinned release is\nmissing** from `/home/coder/.local/share/code-server`, and starts it with\n`--auth none --port 13337 --app-name \"VS Code Web\" --ignore-last-opened <folder>`,\npassing no settings payload. The guard tests\n`/home/coder/.local/share/code-server/lib/code-server-<version>/bin/code-server`,\nthe directory the installer unpacks, because `bin/code-server` beside it is a\nsymlink to whatever release was installed last: a guard on that symlink would keep\na warm volume running its first release after a bump. A bump therefore re-downloads\non the next start and leaves the previous release under `lib/`; removing that old\ndirectory by hand is what reclaims the space. Before that launch it `mkdir -p`s the\nfolder and, when the folder is not `/home/coder`, waits up to 60 s for\n`<folder>/.git` so a first-start\nclone and the editor no longer race. That wait loop increments with\n`waited=$((waited + 1))` (one dollar) inside the tftpl: `templatefile()` interpolates\n`${…}` and leaves `$((` alone. `$$((` would expand `$$` to the PID. `coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the agent would\nchdir there before `startup.sh`, and a missing clone folder would fail the agent. The\neditor's settings live on the per-owner `vscode-settings-<owner-id>` volume seed, not\nin a per-start merge."],[1204,"The `x-litellm-model-id` response header names the deployment that answered."],[1540,"The 195k needle. `//scripts:spark-verify` runs the recipe's `needle.py --length 195000`\nas the window proof, and a 195k prompt the server has never seen is the largest single\neviction on the box. It now skips itself while `/health` reports `requests_running`\nabove zero, so it does not land on a box that is serving:"],[1100,"The Agent backend card"],[117,"The agent runs `coder_agent.startup_script`, and every `coder_script.script`, as\n`bash -c <body>`. The whole body therefore travels as **one** argument to `execve(2)`,\nand Linux caps a single argument at `MAX_ARG_STRLEN`, 32 pages = 131072 bytes. Measured\non this host: an argument of 131071 bytes execs and one of 131072 fails with `E2BIG`.\nPast the wall the agent logs `fork/exec /bin/bash: argument list too long` with\n`exit_code=255` a few milliseconds in, the home directory never seeds, and the workspace\nbanner reads \"A startup script has failed\". Coder puts no limit there and the plan\nreports no error, so the wall only announces itself on a live recreate."],[1086,"The alias table"],[1110,"The alias table, and which backend each alias resolves to"],[1728,"The answer for every `gh` call, for as long as that workspace lives:"],[1741,"The assistant also opens in your own terminal with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-shell`. When you cannot tell whether the agent,\nits volume, or the model lane is the broken part, start with the readiness\nreport — it launches nothing and names each blocker (daemon, container state,\nseeded volume, model key set or still a placeholder, LiteLLM\n`/health/liveliness`):"],[1066,"The assistant on the host"],[588,"The Bazel job no longer installs the pins onto the runner: the image already has them at\nthe pinned versions."],[390,"The blank first value is git's reset marker: it drops any helper inherited from a\nlower-precedence config file, and git takes the **last** value it finds for that host.\nThe startup seed rewrites both keys every start (idempotent), and `github-auth fix`\ninstalls the same form, leaving a helper that already ignores the pair alone. This\nhelper is the slot that wins, and the clearing is the subject of the paragraph below."],[1047,"The box deliberately runs **two isolated copies** of the stack: a stable one you work in\nand a dev one you break. Two stacks on one box\nin the README is the short version; docs/config-updates.md covers\nwhat a `git pull` leaves behind."],[1141,"The box-side reclaim switch. The recipe measured the same refusal going from\n\"2 of 5 streams refused\" to \"5 of 5 admitted\" once patch 0029 is live with\n`TF_FLASHNEXT_ADMIT_RECLAIM=1`. `//scripts:spark-configure --apply` owns the per-host\noverride file those settings belong in, and `//scripts:spark-verify` already fails a\nbox whose `streams.max` drifts from the fleet's `PARALLEL`. Asserting the\nadmit-reclaim setting itself is still open work: neither script names that key today."],[453,"The callback URL on the GitHub side is\n`<CODER_ACCESS_URL>/external-auth/<id>/callback` - `github` for the injected\nApp, `primary-github` for the sample app above."],[1203,"The card and its per-box widgets read `AGENT_MODEL_URL` and `SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` from\n`.env`, so they follow the LAN, and they answer without a key."],[317,"The chat agent discovers its context through its *default* paths (verified against the\nv2.36.6 agent binary and its `agentcontextconfig` source): the instruction file\n`AGENTS.md` under `~/.coder`, skills under `~/.coder/skills`, and a working-dir-relative\n`.agents/skills`, which here resolves to `/home/coder/.agents/skills` - the same folder\nthe Grok Build CLI looks in."],[912,"The chat agent is compiled into coderd unconditionally."],[1740,"The Chat tab is a PTY over WebSocket on the same port. Approvals render as\ncards there; a mutating command that seems to hang is waiting for the card.\nSeeded `approvals.mode` is `manual` — never `off`. `hermes-init` must exit 0\nbefore `hermes` starts. If the gateway loops on a missing `config.yaml`, the\nnamed volume was not seeded: run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-bootstrap -- --help` and recreate. Full\naccess / mounts / socket-risk notes: hermes.md."],[979,"The Chat tab is the door for someone at a browser. The terminal door is for someone\nalready on the box, and it opens the **same** assistant - same `SOUL.md`, same seeded\n`config.yaml`, same git-tracked skills, same LiteLLM lane, same memory, same manual\napproval gate:"],[1572,"The checks that apply:"],[296,"The chown of `~/.grok` and `~/.shared` runs a second time at the end of the script,\nbecause the CLI installer and the seeds above may have created files as root when the\nagent ran as root."],[1298,"The CLI never creates the workspace itself. It prints\n`next: bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <url>` and stops, because `new-workspace.sh` owns the\ndev-container probe and the template choice, and a second copy of that decision would eventually\ndisagree with the first."],[603,"The CLI: bazel-map"],[294,"The clone folder is created before git runs, and before the `coder_script.code_server`\nscript's own `mkdir`: the editor starts in parallel with the startup script, and an\nempty tree is a folder VS Code can open while the clone is still writing. An empty\ndirectory is also what `git clone` accepts, so pre-creating it does not change what gets\ncloned - the emptiness guard still refuses a folder holding anything."],[269,"The clone is a plain `git clone` from the host, so it consults the host's own git\ncredentials. A private repository therefore needs a grant covering it **on the machine\nyou run this from**."],[360,"The code-server module is deliberately given no settings map in plain mode: its merge\nruns on every start and `mv`s over that symlink, which would fork a private copy and\ndiscard the sharing. (`devcontainer` passes the seed to the module only in the mode where\nthe merge is wanted, and bakes it as `settings_b64` for the built container.)"],[57,"The Coder Agents seeds and their agents-tree twins"],[316,"The Coder Agents seeds under `~/.coder` and `~/.agents` are not on that mount: they live\non the workspace's own home volume, and that stage neither needs nor disturbs it."],[792,"The commands are the README's spin-up section\nwith the reasoning attached. Run them on the stack host, not inside a workspace: a\nworkspace's `docker` addresses its own DinD sidecar, and the whole-stack targets reach the\ndaemon that runs Coder."],[1364,"The composed names"],[1063,"The concepts behind the box — what the templates build, how a model request is routed, what the agents file seeds, and the rules a change follows."],[719,"The consequence for a test author: a function counts when something calls it. A\nname in a comment, a stub, a fixture or a quoted grep pattern satisfies\n`//tests:shell_inventory` and satisfies nothing here."],[576,"The contributor container is its own credential chain, so the doctor runs in SELF mode\nthere — exactly as it does inside a Coder workspace:"],[480,"The contributor path"],[487,"The contributor path — the dev container, or `test-in-image` — and where it fits"],[1121,"The control-plane chat agent dials the same proxy at `http://litellm:4000/v1`, over the\ncompose network, from inside the `coder` container. That is the server-side door."],[1194,"The conversation goes back to the box once it is healthy again."],[635,"The cost of not querying shows up late. In the sessions that hit a coupling rule, the first\n`FAIL: //` line appeared only after a hundred or more messages of editing: the rule was\ndiscoverable in the graph on message 1, and was instead discovered by running the gate. One agent\naudited `//:stack_data` by listing the repo's top-level directories rather than diffing the graph\nagainst `git ls-files`, which is the same question asked in a way that cannot answer it."],[1250,"The coupling runs one way: this repo may name the scaffold repository; the scaffold repository\nnever names this one. And the scaffold's quality gate is its own -- the `.self-check/` suite and\nthe CI that runs it in that repository -- so a scaffold change is tested where it is made, and a\nlane or a file the scaffold adds needs no change here."],[389,"The credential helper configured for `https://github.com`. Both template twins seed\n`~/.gitconfig` at startup, for `github.com` and `gist.github.com` alike:"],[1287,"The credential here is the Coder External Auth GitHub App **user-to-server token**; this stack\nnever introduces a static token or PAT. An App can be granted one endpoint and refused another, so\nthe CLI runs the publish side as independent steps, each reporting `ok:` or `warning:` on its own.\nNo step is fatal, because aborting on the first refusal would abandon the steps that were allowed."],[69,"The credential ladder, in git's consult order"],[356,"The custom icon `templates/common/vscode-alt.svg` travels as a\n`data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from `templates/common/`. Coder serves icons\nonly from its bundled `/icon/*` set or from a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is\nnot bundled. `workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a\ncompact ribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`. `push-template` rejects\nthat locally, before the CLI runs."],[231,"The daemon image is resolved with `data \"docker_image\"`, which inspects the host store\nand **never pulls**, so `docker:29.8.1-dind` must already be there. `bootstrap.sh`\npre-arms it; to do it manually:"],[228,"The daemon's `/var/lib/docker` lives on its own volume (`coder-<wsid>-dind-lib`), so a\nstop/start does not re-pull every image the builds used. It is not gated on\n`start_count`, so it survives a stop and is reused by the next start."],[488,"The daily commands, and the Makefile shim over them"],[1201,"The dashboard's **Agent backend** card exists to surface exactly that failure."],[1379,"The default shape is LAN-only. `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` additionally serves the same\nfive services on public names over real TLS, each name composed from the parts you set\nonce. It is purely additive: every LAN route keeps working."],[825,"The delegation sketch"],[485,"The dependency maps and their gate"],[1031,"The deploy workspace is writable by the assistant's file tools only when you say so\nwith `--writable`, and the file tools refuse anything outside the image's\n`HERMES_WRITE_SAFE_ROOT` (the official default is `/opt/data`) with no prompt at all.\nName the narrowest directory that finishes the job."],[1673,"The dev container must build first, and that needs `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`\nin the cloned repository."],[1394,"The dev row is what `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack -- up` composes for the\n`evo-t1-dev` project: that command applies `STACK_ENV_LABEL=dev` when neither the shell\nnor the env file already sets a label, and only for that project, so\n`--project-name evo-t1-review` gets none. A label goes on every name, so that stack's\ndashboard is `dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` too, and all five need their own Traefik\nrouters (see below). `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` never invents a label."],[1217,"The dev sandbox takes the same restart with its own project name and env file:"],[240,"The devcontainer CLI always bind-mounts the workspace folder into the dev container it\ncreates, and a bind source is resolved by the daemon that *runs* that container - here\nthe sidecar, not the workspace's."],[660,"The direct inputs are the `data` the dependency maps write, which is what you edit when a target\nneeds one more payload group:"],[278,"The Docker **client** toolchain only (`docker`, `docker buildx`, `docker compose` as\nCLI plugins under `/usr/local/lib/docker/cli-plugins`). No daemon: the build asserts\n`dockerd`, `containerd`, `runc` and `docker-proxy` are absent."],[177,"The docker provider resolves `evo-t1-dev:latest` from the local image store and never\nattempts a registry pull, which is what keeps workspace creation working with no\ninternet."],[772,"The docker socket stays unmounted: service status comes from each service's API, which\navoids handing the dashboard a root-equivalent host credential. The Hermes card in the\n**Host** group is the same pattern (`siteMonitor` of `http://hermes:9119/api/status`);\nthe Hermes container itself *does* mount the socket — that credential lives there, not\nhere. See hermes.md."],[847,"The editor opens on `http://localhost:13337/?folder=<urlencoded>`: the\n`coder_app` resource in `coder/coder` `~> 2.18` has no `folder` argument, so the\nfolder rides in the URL and as a positional argument to the code-server binary\nwith `--ignore-last-opened`. `workspace` uses `/home/coder/workspace/<repo>` (and\n`/home/coder` with no repository); `devcontainer` uses `/workspaces/<repo>` inside a\nbuilt dev container and `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo>` on the\nplain container. On first start the inlined `coder_script.code_server` creates that\nfolder and waits up to 60 s for `.git` (skipped when the folder is `/home/coder`) so\nthe clone can land before code-server launches. `coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the\nagent would chdir there before `startup.sh`, and a missing clone folder would fail\nthe agent. With the sample `REPO_URL` that folder is\n`/home/coder/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`."],[613,"The empty result is not an answer"],[590,"The equivalent run on your own machine is the host ingress above, so a CI-only failure is\nreproducible without a Linux box or a workflow rerun."],[375,"The extras are cloned **beside** the primary repository, into sibling folders, by the\nsame clone-once rule (a folder that already holds a `.git` is left alone on later\nstarts, never pulled on)."],[1332,"The file header and the function docstring block both gates require"],[743,"The file probe (`-f \"${candidate}/${marker}\"`) says \"this is a repo root\"; refusing a derived\ncandidate whose `pwd -P` lands under `bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `execroot/`, or a\n`*.runfiles` tree says \"and it is the real checkout, not a copy\", because runfiles carries its\nown copy of the marker. Two probes, because either one alone passes in the wrong case: writing a\nrotated `.env` or a `sparks/<host>.env` through the runfiles copy reports success while the\ncheckout keeps the old bytes. An explicit `${REPO_ROOT}` is exempt from the second probe — it is\na statement about where to work, and this harness points it at the copy of the repo under test."],[607,"The filegroup that must claim a new payload file"],[661,"The files it can actually open are the transitive closure of those groups, which is what the verb\nprints:"],[1017,"The first `compose up` runs `hermes-init`, which is the in-container equivalent of\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-bootstrap`: it runs\n`scripts/hermes-bootstrap.sh --in-container` and copies `hermes/config.yaml` and\n`hermes/SOUL.md` into `/opt/data` **only when those files are missing**. Editing the\ncopy in the volume is how an operator ends up with a config no one can reproduce."],[1758,"The first registered account is the site admin."],[279,"The five offline MCP servers ship in the image, but the rendered config declares none\nof them by default, so a workspace needs no network to start a session."],[70,"The four commands"],[1137,"The four groups are independent and none inherits `agent`, so each needs its own six\nkeys. The zeros pin the exceptions `DefaultRetries` would otherwise reach; at this tag\n`NotFoundError` has no field of its own, so without a policy a typo'd model id is\nretried on `DefaultRetries`."],[393,"The gh auth store (`~/.config/gh`, 0600): the one copy on disk, maintained by\n`gh_token_refresh`. Reachable only past a helper that clears the pair."],[1718,"The grant behind the credential - the GitHub App installation that the External Auth\nprovider (id `github` by default) minted its token from - has to include the repository\nthe workspace clones or pushes: GitHub -> Settings -> Applications -> `<app>` ->\n**Configure** -> Repository access."],[814,"The Grok Build CLI in the golden image"],[830,"The Grok Build CLI in the golden image, and how it is pinned"],[54,"The Grok Build CLI probe, the download, and the installer stub"],[1186,"The Grok CLI forwards `extra_headers` verbatim."],[123,"The guard also says the number while it is still a warning: a body within\n`STARTUP_SIZE_WARN` (4096) of the bound prints its composed total and the bytes left,\nbecause the distance to the budget is what a reviewer needs and a hard failure only\never reports the crossing. Measured on 2026-11-03, after the stage comments were\ncollapsed to a pointer here, the widest body is the devcontainer startup script at\nabout 118.5 KB, roughly 4.4 KB clear of the bound; the workspace twin sits near 116.3 KB."],[756,"The header tiles: CPU, memory, temperature, disk, interface throughput"],[978,"The Homepage card in the **Host** group links to `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the public\norigin when set, otherwise the LAN URL). Its `siteMonitor` is\n`http://hermes:9119/api/status`, which resolves over compose DNS, so the card stays\ncorrect whatever the browser link is. See dashboard.md."],[746,"The Homepage dashboard and Kasm Workspaces: what each card reads, how the password is issued, and how a desktop gets streamed."],[848,"The icon travels as a `data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from\n`templates/common/vscode-alt.svg`: Coder serves icons only from its bundled\n`/icon/*` set or a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is not bundled.\n`workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a compact\nribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`."],[777,"The image ships default users (`admin@kasm.local` / `user@kasm.local`); change them\nduring the wizard."],[549,"The image ships the Docker client only. The build ends with `test ! -x\n/usr/local/bin/dockerd` (plus `containerd`, `runc`, `docker-proxy`), so the merge-gate\ncontainer drives a daemon it is given rather than owning one."],[63,"The inlined code-server fork"],[300,"The install check is `command -v grok`, not `[ -x ~/.grok/bin/grok ]`: the golden image\nbakes the CLI into `/usr/local/bin`, so on a golden-image boot the probe answers yes and\nnothing downloads. The download exists for the **Minimal shell** (`ubuntu:24.04`) preset\nand for a home volume that never received a CLI."],[709,"The inventory strips comment lines before it looks for a name, so a name that only ever\nappears in a `# @function` block or in prose is still untested: it has to sit in a code line\nsomewhere under `tests/`. The usual way to get one there is a unit case that calls the\nhelper directly:"],[1220,"The IPEX-LLM runtime is Intel's XPU path (oneAPI / Level Zero) for the Arc stack."],[994,"The key is `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY`, substituted from the environment into the seeded\n`config.yaml`. Do not copy the key into a Hermes file - one secret, one owner."],[306,"The key is what makes the rule hold. The CLI's own settings writer (`grok mcp add` and\nfriends) deletes every comment line, so a comment-only marker can vanish with nobody\ntouching the file."],[794,"The LAN address you will browse to. `CODER_ACCESS_URL` has to be an `https://` URL that\nis not `localhost`, or a workspace cannot reach LiteLLM."],[773,"The layout follows the upstream README example - logo plus greeting top-left, boxed\nresource tiles, clock, weather and search top-right, cards on a wallpaper. The\nwallpaper (`background.webp`) and the logo (`evo-t1.svg`) live in\n`homepage/assets/icons/`, mounted at `/app/public/icons` and served as `/icons/...`.\nHomepage's auth middleware exempts only that path from the sign-in redirect, which is\nwhat lets the same wallpaper render on the login page."],[690,"The layout: suites, helpers, stubs and fixtures"],[1560,"The lever left, not pulled: `[session] auto_compact_threshold_percent` (75 in both\ntemplate twins) decides where auto-compaction fires, so lowering it shrinks the\n~200k-token summary input. The store shows no size threshold separating failure from\nsuccess - the successes reach the same 197k-214k range - so this is a load reduction to\ntry if hangs persist, not a proven cure. Re-run the first command above to measure\nwhether the ratio moved."],[403,"The lines `check` prints are documented with the failures they explain, in\ntroubleshooting.md:\n`git would send this credential (source: ...)`, `NO GitHub credential ...`,\n`[shadowed: ...]`, `push would WORK` / `push would FAIL`, and the dead-`GIT_ASKPASS` note.\n`gh` refusing a healthy store is its own entry there, at\n`gh` answers `Bad credentials`,\nand a workspace with no `gh` binary at\n`gh: command not found`."],[419,"The long form of the `.env.sample` comments: GitHub external auth, Grok profile mode, public hosts, the Spark `agent` group"],[608,"The map key a new `scripts/*.sh` needs, and the test a new function needs"],[1102,"The measured burst"],[890,"The memory and index files are SQLite, opened live by every workspace holding the\nmount, so two workspaces writing memory at once can race. *Roughly in sync* is the\nbar, not transactional sharing."],[540,"The merge gate (Linux image) versus the convenience path (native Darwin)"],[711,"The merge gate is the Linux golden image — the same `images/dev/Dockerfile` the Coder\nworkspaces boot and CI builds:"],[582,"The merge gate is the Linux image, so run it in the contributor container:"],[724,"The mirror pads every `.sh` it instruments, so a suite that measures a payload's\n**byte size** through `REPO_ROOT` prices the harness instead of the product. This is\nhow `//tests:shell_coverage` failed an execve budget the checkout had already passed:\n`templates/common/notes-setup.sh` weighed 1182 bytes more in the mirror than it\nships. A size gate resolves its tree through `bats_source_repo_root`, and\n`startup-script-size.bats` builds a real instrumented mirror to prove it still\nmeasures what ships."],[1065,"The model lane"],[1492,"The model lane: Spark boxes, LiteLLM, and the agent backends"],[1080,"The model lane: the aliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, how a request picks a backend, and what happens when a Spark box goes down."],[1165,"The model writes the JSON call into the message body, which no agent driver can consume."],[964,"The models it calls through LiteLLM"],[257,"The mount covers the workspace container only; a dev container built on the sidecar\nkeeps its own home either way."],[468,"The names also go into `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` (compose appends this raw value)\nand into the Coder certificate's subjectAltName."],[1372,"The naming rule, and what `auto` composes from the parts you set"],[1708,"The note line, `note: GIT_ASKPASS is set but answers no credential, which is what turns\na refused token into \"Password authentication is not supported for Git operations.\"; a\nworking credential helper makes it moot`, is the second half of the story. The agent env\nexports `GIT_ASKPASS` pointing at the `coder` binary the agent staged under\n`/tmp/coder.<rand>/`, and in a measured workspace that binary answered every prompt, and\n`coder gitaskpass` itself, with `unrecognized subcommand` and no output, so git got no\nanswer at all and fell through to prompting. Clearing `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` helps\nonly while the helper answers; with a silent helper it changes nothing."],[715,"The npm-backed gates (`//docs-site:typecheck`, `:unit`, `:unit_coverage`, `:verify`) are\n`manual` for the same reason, and they all run out of `docs-site/node_modules` in the\ncheckout: `run_npm.sh` resolves its own runfiles copy back into it, so a cold checkout\ninstalls once for the whole family, under `docs-site/.npm-ci.lock`. Without that lock\nevery test action ran its own `npm ci`, and a gate that started while another was still\nwriting the tree died on a SIGBUS reading a short esbuild binary. A box with no `flock`\n(a macOS laptop) installs unlocked, since nothing else is running there."],[951,"The on-host assistant with two doors - the Web Dashboard on :9119 and the host terminal via hermes-shell: how to reach it, what it may touch, and how approvals gate a mutating command from either."],[1697,"The one command does the same three steps and then proves them:"],[1702,"The one command, run where the push was attempted, naming the repository:"],[1687,"The one command, whichever of the two it was:"],[1109,"The one front door every client dials, and why nothing else is published"],[528,"The one image, the one pin file"],[527,"The one Linux golden image behind laptop work, the Coder workspaces and CI: what the contributor dev container builds, how it is seeded, and which path is the merge gate."],[31,"The one-argument ceiling"],[1108,"The one-front-door rule"],[786,"The one-time host bootstrap, and why it is the only step run straight from a shell"],[1551,"The only live bound was the proxy's `request_timeout: 1200`, multiplied by every\nattempt. Observed failure durations were 1366 s, about 2400 s and 3465 s - the deadline\nhonoured once, twice and three times."],[292,"The order above is not incidental; each line is a constraint the script has to keep."],[1069,"The order that makes the pieces click, if you are reading them all"],[1425,"The original Host header preserved - a rewritten one comes back as `421`."],[1249,"The other *template* is a **project scaffold**: a tree of files that `scripts/new-repo.sh` copies,\nsubstitutes, and hands to `git init` as the first commit of a brand new repository. It is not\nvendored here. It lives in its own repository -- by default `https://github.com/toxicoder/ezrepo`\n-- and this repo's whole job is to fetch that tree and render it. The scaffold is a product with\nits own CI and its own release rhythm, and a vendored copy drifts from the day it is committed, so\nnothing here is kept in step with it."],[331,"The parent folder (`/home/coder/workspace`, or the `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<wsid>` bind\nin `devcontainer`) is chowned before git runs, and *non-recursively*: other clones live\nalongside this one and are not ours to take over. The clone folder itself is chowned too,\nor a root agent leaves it root-owned and the clone dies writing `.git` inside it."],[765,"The password gate is homepage v2's built-in one, and it is worth keeping: the page\nlists every service on the box. `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` holds the plaintext, because\nhomepage hashes that env var itself but still needs the value."],[757,"The password gate, the proxy in front of it, and how to rotate the password"],[1749,"The paste-ready Traefik file (one backend at `:8080`, `passHostHeader: true`,\nAuthelia on the four interactive names, LiteLLM without Authelia) is in\npublic-access.md#traefik-configuration."],[1363,"The path a public request takes"],[1371,"The path a public request takes, from the browser to the container"],[382,"The path is Coder's external-auth broker. With no `coder.env`, Coder CE injects\na GitHub App whose id is `github`, and both templates declare that id by\ndefault. A custom OAuth app lives in `coder.env` (beside `docker-compose.yml`);\n`push-template.sh` forwards `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` so the templates declare\nthe same id. Link that provider to **both** templates."],[1093,"The picker: least-busy"],[1181,"The pin idles out after 1 h (3600 s) without a request, and every request refreshes it.\nIt bounds idle time between requests, not conversation length."],[1193,"The pin itself is kept."],[1094,"The pin: session_affinity"],[1138,"The policy is scoped to the Spark lanes. `coder` (Arc) and `chat` keep the plain\n`num_retries` path."],[208,"The provider requires a parameter default to be one of its options, which is why a\ncustom `--var` value is injected as its own option instead of being dropped."],[1206,"The proxy's own `/health` lists every deployment, so trust it over the absence of\nboot errors."],[1743,"The public backend is the same hop as the other names. Replay it from a\nworkspace, which reaches the published router and skips Cloudflare, Traefik and\nAuthelia:"],[1024,"The public name is opt-in (`hermes-*`, which `auto` composes from the naming parts or\nan explicit list pins). It does not change what the container can do: local terminal,\nhost mounts and the docker socket still mean this agent can affect the real host.\nTreat its approvals the way you treat a root shell. That is the same standing as\npublic Coder."],[1161,"The Qwen2.5-Coder weights do not emit structured tool calls on this runtime."],[620,"The raw `bazelisk query` predicates for when a verb does not fit"],[831,"The rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`: model aliases and role fallbacks"],[815,"The rendered `~/.grok` config"],[1704,"The repair is also one command, and every `push would FAIL` line names it:"],[514,"The repository cache is the one worth persisting in CI, and `EVO_T1_BAZEL_CACHE=<host dir>`\nis the seam: `//scripts:test-in-image` bind-mounts that directory into the gate container and\npoints `--repository_cache` at it, so each external archive downloads once per key. Each CI\ngate step exports it as `${RUNNER_TEMP}/bazel-repo-cache` and is wrapped in an `actions/cache`\npair keyed on `MODULE.bazel`, `MODULE.bazel.lock` and `.bazelrc`, with the save under\n`if: always()` — the archives fetched\nbefore a red gate are exactly what the next run would otherwise pay for again. That endpoint\nmatters: `github.com/.../releases/download/...` answers 504 for a slice of requests, one such\n504 aborts Bazel's analysis, and a refetch-on-every-job job decides its colour by luck. The\nin-container path is passed as an explicit option because the `~` in `.bazelrc` expands from\nthe Bazel server's own `user.home`, which this container's uid does not appear in: a mount\nplaced from that `~` could sit where Bazel never writes and look like a working cache for\nexactly one run. With the variable unset the script mounts nothing, which is what a\ncontributor laptop gets."],[291,"The repository leaf is derived the same way in `main.tf` and in `startup.sh.tftpl`:\nsplit on `/`, drop empty segments, take the last, drop a trailing `.git`, map every\ncharacter outside `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` to `-`, and fall back to `repo` when that leaves no\nleading alphanumeric. Both derivations must agree, or the editor and the terminal\nhelpers point at a folder that was never cloned."],[327,"The repository URLs arrive as rendered literals and are read through a *quoted* heredoc,\nso no shell quoting has to survive and the value is only ever expanded as data. An empty\nGit repository field means a home-directory workspace, so the whole clone stage is\nskipped rather than attempted with an empty URL."],[991,"The result fails closed: no credentials, no dashboard. There is no unauthenticated\nread-only mode to fall back to."],[1413,"the router's vhost table: `proxy/router.conf.template` is rendered into\n`proxy/router.gen.conf` (gitignored) and `STACK_ROUTER_CONF=router.gen.conf` is\nwritten, because compose mounts `./proxy/${STACK_ROUTER_CONF:-router.conf}`. The\n`warn_missing_router_hosts` check runs against that rendered file, so a name the\ntable does not answer is reported before the recreate rather than as a `421` after\nit."],[1382,"The router's whole job is picking an upstream from the Host header, and an unmatched\nHost answers `421` rather than a guess, so a mistyped or rewritten Host fails loudly\ninstead of landing in the wrong service. A rewritten Host matches no server block\nhere, so it also arrives as `421`."],[658,"The rule class is the part that saves the round trip. `//docs-site:app` looks like the site's test\ntarget and is a `filegroup`, so `bazelisk test` on it fails to start; the real test targets are the\n`sh_test` rows. The same listing filtered by a name fragment is how you find a Bats suite's target\nwithout knowing the stem convention:"],[1260,"The rule that ties it together: **a check that silently skips is a bug**. Two existing\nrepositories print `tool not found ... Skipping` and then exit zero, and a pre-commit config in\nanother wraps every hook in `command -v ... || exit 0`. Nothing in the scaffold may do that, and\nthe scaffold's own `.self-check/` suite is what holds the line."],[1067,"The rules a change follows"],[1283,"The run exits 0 when the render succeeded -- a refused GitHub step is a `warning:`, not a failure --\nand 1 on a render failure, a bad argument, or a scaffold that cannot be fetched."],[725,"The same \"empty looks like green\" rule applies to the vitest gate: it must measure\nfiles no test imported. Vitest 4 made that the default and removed the `all` key\nfrom `CoverageOptions`. To prove the default still holds after a vitest bump, drop\na `lib/__cov_probe.ts` exporting one uncalled function and confirm the run reports\nit at 0% and fails; then delete the file."],[1522,"The same command aimed at the dev stack, so with `dev-stack` and its default\nproject name (`evo-t1-dev`):"],[1503,"The same command with `dev-stack` brings the dev project up. A leftover\n`homepage-proxy` container from the failed create is recreated on that `up`."],[1515,"The same MagicDNS-only resolver is what makes Terraform inside the `coder`\ncontainer SERVFAIL `registry.terraform.io` (`127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving`).\nThat spelling lives under \"Terraform and the host `coder` CLI\" below. Compose\npins public resolvers on `coder` so a template push does not wait on this host\nfix."],[1607,"The same probe in the other spelling: `test -x ~/.grok/bin/grok`."],[37,"The same push, by hand"],[1267,"The scaffold is fetched before anything is rendered: a shallow (`--depth 1`), single-branch clone\nof `--template-ref` into a scratch directory an EXIT trap removes. `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` and an\nempty credential helper keep a scaffold that turned private from hanging on a prompt nobody can\nanswer inside a workspace. A fetched tree with no `template.yml` at its root is a hard failure --\nthat manifest belongs to the scaffold repository, so pick a ref that has it. The new repository's\nfirst commit records what it was built from: `- Scaffold: <url>@<sha>.`, or\n`- Scaffold: local tree <dir>.` for a `--template-dir` render."],[1301,"The scaffold's `.self-check/` suite, in that repository and run by its own CI, owns the template's\ninternal invariants: every token the template uses is one this script declares, every lane ships\nits `LANE.md` plus `gitignore.fragment` pair, every tool is pinned rather than `@latest`, the\nworkflows declare `permissions`, the docs links resolve, and the pins cover the lanes present."],[524,"The scan treats a comment, a here-doc body, a log message, and a scratch file a suite writes\nas prose rather than a dependency, so the maps record reads and not mentions. When the scan\ngenuinely cannot see a pairing (a lib that builds a path and returns it as a string, a\n`case` glob over `git diff` output, help text), the entry goes in the `_ALLOW` map with the\nreason as a comment above the key; the Bats gate fails an `_ALLOW` key that has no reason."],[141,"The script mints a short-lived token by inserting a row into the stack's own Postgres."],[833,"The search lane and the MCP servers behind it"],[369,"The seed also pins `terminal.integrated.cwd` and `terminal.integrated.splitCwd` to the\nopened folder, adds a `terminal.integrated.env.linux` block, and carries a\n`commandsToSkipShell` list - all described in the sibling doc."],[361,"The seed in git carries no API key. Cline and Roo read their key out of `settings.json`\nand have no `env_key` field, so when `LITELLM_API_KEY` is set the startup script injects\nit into the seed once (`jq`, falling back to `python3`) and nowhere else."],[1752,"The seed sets `dashboard.trusted_proxies` to `[\"172.16.0.0/12\"]` so Hermes\nhonours `X-Forwarded-Proto` (Secure cookie and `wss`). A `hermes-data` volume\nfrom before that seed keeps the old file until you reseed. `<mount>` is the\nhost directory of that volume:"],[1012,"The seeded config sets `approvals.mode: manual`. `cron_mode` and `unattended_mode` are\nboth `deny`, so a scheduled or unattended run cannot approve anything either."],[948,"The seeds live per workspace, on that workspace's own home volume. They are *not*\non the shared `~/.grok` profile volume above, and seeding does not care whether\nthat mount exists."],[1576,"The self-hosted SearXNG MCP stdio server is the search lane, declared by default\n(`search_mcp` defaults to true). Grok's native `web_search` is parked: the render sets\n`disable_web_search = true`, because no model transport this box ships serves that\ntool type - LiteLLM's `agent` group answers `tools of type 'web_search' are not\nsupported: this server runs function tools only` (HTTP 400) before anything is\nfetched, and `supports_backend_search = false` does not route around it. An agent that\nstill spends a turn on a native search call and comes back with that message behaved\nas configured; there is no native lane to fall back to. Two probes split a SearXNG\nfault in half:"],[592,"The sentinel outranks every other signal, so `new-workspace` answers `workspace` for this\nrepo and a Coder workspace of it never builds the contributor container on its DinD\nsidecar."],[1534,"The server on each Spark box logs four shapes, and three of them are this stack's own\ntraffic rather than anything wrong with the box. Read the shape before you reach for\nthe box."],[172,"The service appears twice by necessity: the first `coder` selects the compose service, the\nsecond is the CLI binary inside it."],[872,"The seven aliases (`agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`, `agent-xhigh`, `coder`,\n`coder-fast`, `chat`) are defined with `base_url` set to `litellm_url` and\n`env_key = \"LITELLM_API_KEY\"`. The key is injected as an environment variable, so\nit never appears in a file. `agent` carries three\n`[[model.\"agent\".reasoning_efforts]]` tables (`xhigh` is the default). The\n`agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` aliases set\n`supports_reasoning_effort = false` because LiteLLM bakes the level for callers\nthat have no reasoning-effort field."],[1400,"The shape is load-bearing: each name is **one label under the zone**, and each service\nprefix joins it with a **hyphen**. A wildcard certificate covers exactly one label, so\nthe dotted two-label form (`coder.gmktecbeast.`) has no certificate at the public edge:\nthe browser aborts with `ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` and Traefik never sees the\nrequest. Measured on this box - `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` completes the handshake\nwith the zone's `*.overeazy.io` cert, while the dotted form fails with a TLS alert 40\nand presents no certificate at all."],[56,"The shared grok profile volume"],[1324,"The shell, function, script, template and naming rules this repo holds itself to, and the gates that enforce them."],[1061,"The sidebar tabs divide the rest: **Learn** covers how the pieces work, **Operate** covers\nrunning the box day to day, and **Contribute** covers the gates a change runs through.\nEach subject has exactly one page, and the README links to that page rather than\nrepeating it."],[536,"The single golden image, and the file that pins it"],[339,"The six paths it probes are the ones `@devcontainers/cli` looks in: `devcontainer.json`\nand `devcontainer.yml` at the root, under `.devcontainer/`, and under\n`.github/devcontainers/`."],[118,"The size that matters is not `startup.sh.tftpl`. `main.tf` composes the body from that\ntemplate plus every `templates/common/` payload it inlines, so any edit to the rulebook,\nthe skills, the tmux config, the notes seeder, or the Grok config moves the number. That\nis how six commits crept from 104 KB to 130 KB and one documentation commit crossed the\nwall at 135 KB."],[1300,"The split follows the ownership: the scaffold's own invariants are checked in the scaffold\nrepository, and this repo checks only the renderer."],[1036,"The stack runners take `up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`; `status` and `logs` are\nwhere a Hermes that will not bind its port is diagnosed first. Status across the whole\nbox, including this card, is in dashboard.md; the LAN and public\nrouting rules are in public-access.md, and the service inventory is\nin README.md."],[336,"The stage reads the *rendered* file, pulls every `command = \"...\"` value out of it, and\nnames the ones that are not executable files, in the same startup log that wrote the\nconfig. `sed` keeps only a double-quoted value and a TOML path carries no spaces, so an\nempty entry there is an odd line rather than a missing binary."],[688,"The stub-PATH recipe"],[694,"The stub-PATH recipe, and the two paths a suite may take"],[605,"The suites that read a payload"],[1548,"The summary call rides the **session model**, the `agent*` thinking family, and\ninherits its output ceiling: captured on the wire as `max_tokens: 32768`, alongside 25\ntool schemas and the workspace's `x-litellm-session-id` affinity header.\n`[models] session_summary` is **not** that lane - it serves titles and the session\nrecap."],[170,"The supported push is `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`: no arguments pushes every\ntemplate, a template name retries just that one, and the image tag comes from `DEV_IMAGE`\nin `.env`. The raw equivalent below is what that script does internally — it streams the\nsame tar and makes the same CLI call the script makes for each template, execing into the\ndaemon's `coder` container by name."],[147,"The tar is built from a throwaway staging copy, not from the template dir itself.\n`.terraform/` is excluded (pushing it would put the plan's provider binaries into the\nversion's Filestore row), symlinks are dumped as their targets (`tar -h`), and the\n`templates/common/` payloads are copied in beside `main.tf`. A staged copy that still\nholds a symlink is refused before the CLI runs."],[1445,"The TensorFold log is full of warnings, or `resumed at 0` -> read the line shapes first"],[1030,"The terminal door adds a way in for someone who already has a shell on this host, and\nopens no new listener, no new port and no new credential. `hermes-shell` authenticates\nnothing of its own: `docker` on that host is the credential, and it was always\nroot-equivalent here. Its one guard is that the assistant's approvals stay `manual` on\nboth doors."],[944,"The text of 1-4 is the orchestration protocol (fan-out / fan-in, the edit-conflict gate,\nprobe-one-worker-first, verify-what-a-worker-actually-produced). It travels as\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, staged beside the staged `main.tf` and\nread back with `file()` / `filebase64()`; `templates/common/` keeps one copy for\nboth templates, and the push stages it into each template's pushed copy."],[1154,"The three `agent-*` rows are that same lane with a thinking level baked into\n`litellm_params.reasoning_effort`. Qwen3.8-Flash-Next takes `low`, `medium` and\n`xhigh` and nothing else - there is no `high` - so the level is a name to pick\nrather than a knob to turn, and `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` are\nhow the level reaches Coder Agents: its model dropdown chooses an alias, not a\nrequest parameter."],[1582,"The three MCP servers (`github`, `context7`, `searxng`) ship by default, so a lane\nmissing from a session has one of three shapes: the block absent from the rendered\nconfig, the `command=` binary absent from the golden image, or the credential absent.\nRun `bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` first - it reports the declared lanes, their\nbinaries, their credentials and their handshake in one pass, and it names which shape\nyou are in (an undeclared lane is a failure, a missing binary a warning).\n`grok mcp doctor` inside the workspace lists the declared servers and their handshake.\nA *park* is deliberate and looks like this: `GITHUB_MCP=false` / `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` /\n`SEARCH_MCP=false` in `.env` (relayed by `push-template.sh`) or\n`--var github_mcp=false` / `--var context7_mcp=false` / `--var search_mcp=false` on a\ntemplate push. With none of those set, a missing block is a defect, not a park."],[687,"The three paths a suite may use"],[1282,"The three scaffold flags exist so the source can be pointed elsewhere without editing anything:\nthe env pair covers a mirror or a fork for a whole deployment, and `--template-dir` renders a tree\nyou already hold, which is the offline route and the one the hermetic tests use."],[931,"The thresholds are separate as well. `context_limit` above is Coder's own compaction\nbudget with its 70 per cent default, while the CLI fires at\n`[session] auto_compact_threshold_percent` (75) of its 262144-token window."],[226,"The toggle is `mutable = false`: turning it on or off means a workspace **rebuild**,\nbecause a network, a volume and a second container appear or vanish. The preset makes\nthat one click."],[143,"The token is scoped to minutes (`CODER_PUSH_TOKEN_MINUTES`, default 10), deleted on the\nway out including on a failed or interrupted run, never printed, and minted **once** for\nthe whole run however many templates it covers."],[495,"The toolchain Bazel drives comes from one Linux golden image, `images/dev/Dockerfile`, and\nthe contributor path is that image rather than a set of tools installed on your machine:"],[1184,"The trade is deliberate: stickiness buys prefix-cache locality and one predictable box\nfor the whole conversation, so one workspace's agent loop can sit on a busy box."],[1374,"The Traefik file-provider config the other box needs, pasted as-is"],[469,"The Traefik on the other box owns TLS for those names and must forward plain\nHTTP to this box's router container **with the original Host header intact** -\n`STACK_ROUTER_CONF` names the conf that container mounts (`proxy/router.gen.conf`,\nrendered by bootstrap from `proxy/router.conf.template`, or the checked-in\n`proxy/router.conf` while the key is empty), and it picks the service from\n`$http_host` on `STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT` (default 8080). A rewritten Host comes back\nas `421`.\nThat box needs one file, and the paste-ready version of it - one `loadBalancer`\nat `:8080`, a router per name, Authelia on the four interactive names, the\nlong-lived timeouts - is\npublic-access.md#traefik-configuration."],[606,"The twin template copies"],[1306,"The two commands"],[1310,"The two commands an operator runs most"],[691,"The two inventory gates that tie a production function to a test"],[1140,"The two levers that actually move load off a shedding box:"],[518,"The two maps in `scripts/BUILD.bazel` and the one in `tests/BUILD.bazel` are the\nmachine-readable record of which repo file each target reads:"],[817,"The two modes"],[407,"The two passes"],[416,"The two passes of `update-configs`, and the flags that select one"],[46,"The two questions"],[161,"The two research lanes split by question type: a library, framework, CLI or setup\nquestion goes to `context7` (resolve the library id first, then query docs on that\nid), anything else on the web goes to the `searxng` `search` tool, and a URL already\nin hand goes to `searxng`'s `fetch_content`. Grok's native `web_search` is parked\nhere, and native `web_fetch` is unavailable because that tool needs an egress proxy\nendpoint this stack does not run, so `SEARCH_MCP=false` leaves a session with library\ndocs and no way to reach the web."],[568,"The two research lanes that config can render are split by question type: a library,\nframework, CLI or setup question goes to `context7` (resolve the library id first, then\nquery docs on that id), anything else on the web goes to the `searxng` `search` tool,\nand a URL already in hand goes to `searxng`'s `fetch_content` - Grok's native\n`web_search` is parked here and native `web_fetch` needs an egress proxy endpoint this\nstack does not run, so neither is a lane. Prove what this home declares with:"],[656,"The uncached pass is not replaced. `bazelisk test` replays a cached PASS for an action whose inputs\ndid not change, so the derived list proves your change against whatever actually rebuilds, and one\n`bazelisk test //:test-fast --nocache_test_results` remains the pass you owe before the PR, per\nbuilding-with-bazel.md. Use the derived list for the loop and keep the full\nuncached pass for the end, instead of paying for sixty suites to learn one thing per edit."],[309,"The unmanaged case is announced rather than silent, because the render is the only place\na workspace's model catalog and MCP lanes are ever written."],[1655,"The usual wrapper is `Failed to check server \"https://...\" for first user, is the URL\ncorrect and is coder accessible from your browser?`"],[636,"The verbs below are the shapes of those questions."],[1710,"The verdicts, and what each one costs you:"],[298,"The watcher left at the end is what makes notes survive a recreate that lands *between*\nedits. The boot-time sync runs once, so anything written later lived solely inside that\ncontainer, which is how a whole workspace's notes went missing on a recreate."],[675,"The whole-graph enumeration `unlisted` is built from, which is also the answer to \"how much of the\nrepo do the gates actually see\":"],[1541,"The window is still read passively on every run without the needle, from the server's\nown startup lines (`context_pairs` and `startup_estimate` in the report), so a box\nquietly rebuilt with a shorter window is still visible; the needle is the active\nconfirmation of it. `--no-tests` skips all four recipe probes for a quick read-only\npass, and that is what `//scripts:bootstrap` runs."],[1756,"The wizard only listens on `:3000` at first boot; after install, use `:4443`."],[1517,"The word the shell says it cannot find is the second tag. The same read would\nalso have executed anything written on a line of its own in that file, and it\nexported every other key there (`LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` included) into the\nenvironment of the `docker exec` calls."],[222,"The workspace container attaches to **both** `bridge` and that private network; once a\n`networks_advanced` block appears the provider stops letting `network_mode` do the\nattaching, so `bridge` has to be listed explicitly."],[1574,"The workspace container must resolve `host.docker.internal`; the model lane reaches\nLiteLLM through that host gateway, and so does `LITELLM_BASE_URL`."],[1721,"Then check **how** the credential is sent, because the two GitHub endpoints disagree\nabout the scheme. `api.github.com` and `gh` accept `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. The\ngit data endpoint refuses it: one token answered `/user` with `200` and\n`<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack` with `401` plus\n`remote: invalid credentials` while the token rode a bearer header. git has to send it\nas **Basic** auth with the token as the password. The username text is not checked\n(measured on Coder v2.36.6: `toxicoder`, `x-access-token`, `somebody-else` and `octocat`\neach returned `200`), but an empty username or an empty password is refused, and the\nempty password is what produces the misleading `Invalid username or token. Password\nauthentication is not supported for Git operations.`"],[1720,"Then check the URL scheme, which no probe can fix: that credential covers\n`https://github.com/...` remotes only, so a `git@github.com:` or `ssh://` URL gets\nneither helper nor askpass token and needs an SSH key you placed yourself, which this\nstack provisions nowhere."],[452,"Then connect your GitHub account in Coder (Settings -> Tokens / external auth)\nand link the same provider to **both** workspace templates (Templates -> edit\n-> External Auth)."],[384,"Then connect your GitHub account. On a running workspace, click **GitHub reauth** on\nthe agent bar (Coder v2.36's `/external-auth/<id>` page; use **Reauthenticate** if the\npage says you are already authenticated). First-time connect also works from the\nworkspace create/update form and from Settings -> Tokens / external auth. Device-flow\nlogin is not enough for private repos: install Coder's GitHub App with **Install\nGitHub App** on the same bar (or open\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new),\ngrant those repositories, then restart. An empty\ngithub.com/settings/apps page is not a misconfiguration:\nwith `auth_type=client_secret` Coder has no App to install, and Coder's built-in GitHub\nApp only earns a page there once it is installed on the account. Until something is\ninstalled and granted, that page simply has nothing to **Configure**, and the credential\nthe provider mints still authenticates - it just cannot write to a private repository."],[804,"Then finish the two browser-only steps: run the Kasm wizard once at `https://<host>:3000`\nand use `https://<host>:4443` after it, and open the dashboard at `https://<host>/` with\nthe `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` that `bootstrap` printed."],[797,"Then look before you change anything. `--check` is read-only: missing tools, placeholder\nsecrets, certificates, images."],[1610,"Then open a new terminal; `PATH` there already includes `~/.grok/bin`."],[174,"Then push `workspace` (the repo is not mounted into the container, so the directory has to\ntravel as a tar):"],[180,"Then re-pin both values:"],[1698,"Then read the store with the pair cleared, because `gh auth status` on its own reports\nthe env pair rather than what is stored:"],[1632,"Then run `bash -lc 'command -v grok'`. A workspace on a current image answers\n`/usr/local/bin/grok`, so an empty answer means the CLI is missing, not the profile."],[148,"Then, per template, it confirms an **active** template version. A push can exit 0 and\nstill leave the template inactive if its Terraform run failed, which shows up as the\ntemplate missing from the create dropdown."],[1200,"There are no rpm, tpm or `max_parallel_requests` caps anywhere, on purpose. A cap parks\nrequests behind an in-memory semaphore inside the proxy; without one, a fully offered\nbox sheds load in its own scheduler."],[164,"There is no GitHub-token template variable, so no push ever carries a personal access\ntoken. See GitHub credentials for a workspace."],[1581,"There is no second lane behind this one, so run these probes before touching anything\nelse. A service that answers but a server that will not handshake is the MCP hop; a\nserver that cannot launch at all usually means no\n`/opt/npm-global/bin/searxng-mcp-server`, and that binary is baked into the golden\nimage - an image that predates it needs a rebuild and a workspace recreate, not a\nconfig edit. Park the lane with `search_mcp = false` on the next template push\n(`SEARCH_MCP=false` in `.env` applies it to every new workspace) when a session should\nhave no search tool at all. To restore the native tool, flip `disable_web_search` to\nfalse in both template twins and re-push - only for an endpoint that really serves the\ntool, or the 400 comes straight back."],[1387,"These are what the shipped defaults compose. The operator sets the parts, not these\nstrings: the grammar is `<service>-<environment>-<suffix>.<base>`, always ONE label\nunder the zone."],[1251,"These files belong to the scaffold repository. This repo does not ship them, lint them, or keep a\ncopy: it fetches them at run time and renders them. The scaffold is language-neutral at the core,\nwith optional per-language lanes. Everything below was chosen from what is measurably missing\nacross the account, not from taste: across a sample of eleven ordinary projects, `SECURITY.md`,\n`CONTRIBUTING.md`, issue templates, a pull-request template, `dependabot.yml`, `CODEOWNERS` and\n`.editorconfig` were each present in none of them, and no repository in the surveyed set had a\nsingle release or tag."],[423,"These four sections are also the long form of the `.env.sample` comments that\npoint here: GitHub external auth, Grok profile mode, public hosts, and the\nSpark `agent` model group. Where a topic page already owns a subject, this page\npoints at it instead of restating it."],[1139,"These retries are instant and stay on the same box. A status-less exception means\n`_deployment_ids_to_skip_on_retry` excludes nothing and `_time_to_sleep_before_retry`\nreturns 0 while any deployment is healthy, and the workspace-wide\n`x-litellm-session-id` pin sends every subagent of a workspace to one box anyway. The\nblock makes a refusal less likely to surface. Relocation comes from the box and the\nfan-out."],[1112,"Thinking levels: what `agent` bakes, and what the baked aliases change"],[1657,"This is not a broken credential. It happens with a valid token too, because Authelia\ngates every route."],[641,"This is the question that costs the most sessions, because the answer is spread over four different\nmechanisms and each one is normally discovered by a failing gate."],[678,"This is the trap that makes a hand-check unreliable, and it is the reason the CLI keeps Bazel's\nstderr instead of discarding it. A query can print `ERROR: no such target` and leave stdout empty,\nwhich looks exactly like \"nothing depends on this\":"],[663,"This is why an unlisted payload directory stays invisible rather than being checked loosely: a file\nthat travels in no runfiles tree is a file no sandboxed action can open, so `test-fast` and `lint`\nnever read it and never fail. Nothing prints a warning about that. `owners <path>` and\n`unlisted <dir>` are the two ways to ask, and they are the difference between a payload the harness\nguards and a payload nobody has ever executed."],[1423,"This repo cannot verify any of it; check it on the Traefik box."],[626,"This repo keeps its coupling in BUILD files: `scripts/BUILD.bazel` names the libs and payload\ngroups each CLI reads, `tests/BUILD.bazel` names what each Bats suite opens, and the root\n`BUILD.bazel` aggregates every payload group into `//:stack_data`. `//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test`\nfails when those maps drift from the sources, so the graph is a record an automated reader can\ntrust, and \"which suites cover this file\" has a ground truth sitting in it. Answering that from\n`grep` re-derives what one `bazelisk query` prints."],[1248,"This repository already uses the word *template* for a **Coder template**: the Terraform tree\nunder `templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/` that `push-template.sh` registers with\nthe Coder server."],[1046,"This repository brings up one Compose project on a GMKtec EVO-T1 (Intel Core Ultra 9\n285H, Arc 140T iGPU, 96 GB DDR5) and keeps it runnable by conversation, by dashboard, or\nby `bazelisk`. Everything is a Bazel target addressed as\n`bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`. On the stack host, `scripts/setup-host.sh` is\nthe one script meant to run straight from a shell. On a laptop, reopen the repo in the\ncontributor environment or run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image`."],[347,"Those four are the whole bar. The proxy address reaches the operator as the `litellm`\nitem on the `workspace_info` tile below (`value = var.litellm_url`), a read-out\nrather than a proxied app, so health-checking the proxy is\n`curl http://host.docker.internal:4000/health/liveliness` on the stack host."],[510,"Those operator runs are bounded: a script's non-interactive `docker exec` closes stdin\n(`</dev/null`), and the docker wrappers go through `timeout(1)` with `--kill-after`, so a\n`bazelisk run //scripts:<stem>` cannot sit forever the way `docker exec -i` did under\n`bazelisk run`, waiting for an EOF that never came. `logs --follow` is the intentional\nlong-running exception, and you stop it with Ctrl-C."],[1552,"Those re-dispatches came from this repo's own config. At the pinned tag\n`ghcr.io/berriai/litellm:v1.102.1` a stream that dies before its first chunk raises\n`MidStreamFallbackError`, which subclasses `ServiceUnavailableError`\n(`litellm/exceptions.py:1114`), and `get_num_retries_from_retry_policy` walks the\nexception MRO most-specific first, so it lands on `ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries` and\nnever reaches `DefaultRetries`. A hung request never cools the box either: cooldown\nhard-excludes any exception whose string contains `APIConnectionError`, and\n`_deployment_ids_to_skip_on_retry` only skips deployments that answered a non-retryable\nint status code. Under the workspace-wide `x-litellm-session-id` pin every attempt\ntherefore returned to the same saturated box."],[1156,"Those three groups reuse the same `SPARK1/2/3_OPENAI_URL` / `SPARK_n_API_KEY`\npairs as `agent`. They are separate model groups on the same hardware, not three\nmore boxes: routing state is per group, so least-busy's counters and the session\npin are computed inside a group and two chats on different aliases can land on the\nsame box at once. What does not change is the box - 5 full 262144-token streams in\ntotal, whichever aliases arrive on it. This is not the \"two deployments on one\nbox\" mistake below, which is about a single group counting two URLs on one machine\nas two machines."],[1171,"Three deployments, one per Spark box, and no `order:` tier. A tier would make the\nlower boxes unreachable while any tier-1 box stayed healthy."],[721,"Three runfiles traps bit this gate, and all fail *silently*, so they are worth\nnaming:"],[1635,"Three separate reads, from the stack host. The first prints `unhealthy` rather than\nan error when the daemon fails its own probe, and `no such container` when the\nworkspace has no sidecar at all:"],[716,"Three targets answer \"is this code actually executed by a test\" instead of\nassuming it. They are `manual` like the npm gates, so `//:test-fast` stays fast, and\nthey are members of `//:test-all`, which pre-push and CI run:"],[1568,"Titles that never appear and a long chat that dies at its context budget are the\nhelper lanes pointing at a dead alias. Re-running the script moves them."],[1380,"TLS terminates at Traefik. The `router` sidecar holds no certificate."],[897,"To put native search back on a transport that really serves the tool: flip the key\nto `false` in the seed - both `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl` and\n`templates/devcontainer/`'s copy - and re-push, which applies it fleet-wide. For one\nworkspace without a push, the create form's **Grok config extras** field appends\nfree-form TOML verbatim to that workspace's file. Either way start a new session,\nand expect the same 400 back if the endpoint still answers function tools only."],[1018,"To reseed deliberately:"],[1596,"To take the same reading from inside the workspace, replay the path on `:8080`, the\npublished `router` port. That reaches `coder:3000` through the sidecar while\nbypassing Traefik, Authelia and the WAN, so a reply there that differs from the\nbrowser's `https://` URL names the cloud hops."],[1088,"Tool calls: the measured limitation"],[1111,"Tool calls: the measured limitation and the path that supports them"],[1754,"Traefik and Authelia have to pass `Upgrade`. Cloudflare's orange-cloud proxy\nhas WebSockets on by default. A page that loads and a Chat tab that dies is\nthis hop, not the basic-auth provider."],[1367,"Traefik configuration"],[1434,"Troubleshooting"],[807,"Troubleshooting — start here when a step above says no."],[2,"Trunk-based branch rules: main is the only long-lived branch, work branches are short-lived, and a pull request is the only way back in."],[13,"Trunk-based, GitHub-Flow shaped: one long-lived branch, short-lived work branches, and a pull\nrequest as the only way back into `main`."],[849,"Two buffers are involved: tmux's own buffer, and the clipboard of the computer\nyou are typing on. Which one a copy reaches depends on what sits at the other\nend of the terminal. Each claim below was measured in a headless Chromium\nagainst the code-server this stack shipped then (4.143.1, VS Code base 1.143.1).\nThe pin has since moved to 4.145.0 (VS Code base 1.145.0), and upstream's notes\nfor the two releases in between cover the built-in proxy's cookies, the web\nsocket origin check and the VS Code base bump, with no terminal or clipboard\nentry, so these claims stand as measured and none was re-run\n(v4.144.0,\nv4.145.0)."],[1237,"Two different things called \"template\", and which one this page is about"],[1082,"Two doors, one key"],[673,"Two exclusions earn their place. `except kind(\"test_suite\", //...)` drops the aggregates, which is\nwhat the CLI filters on so `//:test-fast` cannot answer a per-file question, and\n`except kind(\"sh_test\", //lints:*)` drops the two manual lint targets, `//lints:shell` and\n`//lints:shfmt`, that ride on every script:"],[1370,"Two follow-ups, both by hand"],[683,"Two gates, one rule"],[366,"Two helpers are seeded into `~/.grok/bin` on every start, both `chmod +x`:\n`vscode-terminal-tmux` (the default profile) and `grok-build-terminal`."],[1591,"Two hops can answer 502 on that path: this box's nginx, when `coder:3000` will not\nresolve or connect, and Coder's own Go proxy, whose `ReverseProxy` ErrorHandler\nfires when the app upstream answers nothing. Two reads tell them apart, both from\nthe stack host:"],[422,"Two kinds of file go stale in this repo, and because neither of them is\nchecked in, `git pull` cannot fix either one for you. `update-configs` is the\none command that refreshes both, and it is written to be safe to run when you\nare not looking closely at the diff."],[1286,"Two kinds of path are removed on the way out: a lane's reserved metadata (`LANE.md`,\n`gitignore.fragment`, whose contents are consumed rather than copied) and the generator-owned paths\nin `NEW_REPO_TEMPLATE_META`, stripped from every render: `.template-lanes/` (the overlay source this\nscript reads), `.self-check/` (the scaffold's conformance suite, which goes stale the moment a real\nproject diverges from the template it was checked against), and `.git` (which would hand the new\nrepository the template's history and origin remote)."],[1230,"Two kinds of template"],[1143,"Two knobs that look right and do nothing here: `retry_after` is only a `min_timeout` and\nis never consulted while the group has healthy deployments, and `fallbacks_by_model`\ndoes not exist in this tag (the proxy warns and ignores an unknown `router_settings`\nkey, so a config that looks correct silently does nothing, which is why\n`//tests:bats_litellm_config_test` rejects it by name)."],[572,"Two rules decide every write, and they are the same rules the workspace startup applies:"],[761,"Two services that read the box rather than serve code: the Homepage dashboard (the\nlanding page) and Kasm Workspaces (streamed desktops)."],[81,"Two templates live under `templates/`, and a push covers both."],[1074,"Two templates turn a git URL into a running container: the plain `workspace` template, and\n`devcontainer`, which builds the clone's own `.devcontainer` on a DinD sidecar. Both are\npushed from this checkout, and both seed the agent config the workspace starts with."],[888,"Two things about that start are worth knowing before you go looking for a vanished\nsetting. Boot **writes `git config --global core.excludesFile`** — but only when you\nhave not set it yourself. Left unset it points at `~/.grok/notes/exclude`, which lists\n`notes.md` and `.scratch-notes.md`, so from then on *every* git repository in that\nworkspace ignores both names at any depth; if you already set the option, those two\nlines are appended to the file it names and nothing else about it is touched. Boot also\ndrops the same two lines into each clone's own `.git/info/exclude`, which is the layer\nthat works even with no global config at all. And when a repository tracks a `notes.md`\nof its own, the scratch file is written as `.scratch-notes.md` beside it instead — your\nclone is never asked to delete somebody else's committed file."],[119,"Two things follow from the mechanism:"],[1538,"Two things on this stack cause the evictions, and both are tuned here rather than on the\nbox:"],[653,"Two things scope tells you that a habit of `bazelisk test //:test-fast` hides:"],[1295,"Two things, and neither replaces the CLI."],[891,"Two workspaces of one owner that both hold a clone of the *same* repository each run a\nnotes watcher over the same canonical path, so there too the last write wins, tick by\ntick. The stub and empty guards and the `backups/` archive are what bound the damage a\nlosing tick can do; they do not merge two diverging files."],[1485,"UI and logins"],[1746,"Under `auto` both sides come from the same parts, so a `421` on a name nobody\nrecognises is a part problem, not a router problem: an empty `STACK_DOMAIN_BASE`\ncomposes nothing (the stack stays LAN-only) and a mistyped `STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX`\nor `STACK_ENV_LABEL` composes a name no vhost, no DNS and no certificate serves.\nRead the composed list back from `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` in `.env`, fix the part, and\nre-run `bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap`, then recreate. Editing the rendered\n`proxy/router.gen.conf` instead only survives until the next bootstrap, which\nre-renders it from the parts."],[466,"Under `auto` the parts are the source of truth and `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is output\nonly: bootstrap writes the resolved list back and records the intent in\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE=auto`, so a later run re-composes and a list edited by hand\nin `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is ignored. Bootstrap prints a `note:` naming the key to\nclear when that happens."],[722,"Under `bazelisk test` every source arrives as a **symlink**. A `find -type f`\nover the tree then sees no regular file and reports an empty suite, which reads\nas \"nothing to test\". `py_cov.sh` uses `find -L`."],[1071,"Understanding a behaviour before trying to change it"],[175,"Use this shape for reference, or for pushing a tag `.env` does not carry."],[551,"Use VS Code, Cursor, or any editor with the Dev Containers extension, and reopen this\nrepository **in a container**."],[769,"v2.4.0 serves a prerendered page with the settings baked in, so a freshly created\ncontainer shows an empty default layout (`initialSettings {}`) until the config is\nre-read. Click the refresh button in the header, or:"],[1222,"Vanilla Ollama on an Intel iGPU is effectively CPU-bound, and several times slower on a\n32B coder model."],[1476,"VS Code Web opens `/home/coder` instead of the cloned repository -> last workspace won"],[1479,"VS Code Web tab in Chrome creeps to gigabytes, or freezes now and then -> every tab was another mirror of one pane"],[1428,"WebSocket `Upgrade` forwarded by Traefik and Authelia on the Hermes router. The\nChat tab uses `/api/pty` and `/api/ws`, and Hermes sends a keepalive every 20\nseconds. A proxy that drops `Upgrade` loads the page and then fails the tab."],[1373,"What `bootstrap.sh` changes when the names are set"],[1366,"What `bootstrap` changes when the names are set"],[829,"What `grok doctor` reports inside a workspace, and which findings are expected"],[7,"What `main` is allowed to be, and what may not be committed to it"],[538,"What `seed-dev-env` materializes into that home, and what it refuses to touch"],[182,"What a create form shows, in `workspace` order."],[619,"What a target actually reads, and why an unlisted payload directory stays invisible"],[611,"What a target reads, and why an unlisted directory is invisible"],[1090,"What agent mode needs"],[1083,"What an alias is"],[483,"What Bazel brings"],[489,"What Bazel brings over running the scripts directly"],[1095,"What carries a session id"],[533,"What CI does"],[541,"What CI does, and why this repo's own Coder workspaces stay on `workspace`"],[10,"What CI runs, what branch protection should require, and where secrets go"],[1699,"What each answer means: a store that now holds a token means git is repaired and the\nrefresh script keeps it fresh (start, then every 20 minutes); `gh refused the token, so the\nstore is unchanged` means the credential is revoked or the account disconnected, so run\n`check` to see what github.com says about it, then take the clicks from\nthe reauth entry;\n`cannot install the credential helper - gh is not installed` is\nthe gh-not-installed entry."],[755,"What each dashboard card shows and which API it reads"],[1234,"What each GitHub step needs"],[1096,"What floats freely"],[406,"What goes stale"],[405,"What goes stale after a `git pull`, what `update-configs` refreshes, and what it deliberately will not touch."],[129,"What is deliberately not done: bind-mounting `templates/` into the workspace so the\nscript can `cat` a payload off the host, and fetching one over HTTP at boot. The first\ncouples boot to a host path that a template push rewrites, and the second puts a\nnetwork dependency on the path that creates the home directory."],[818,"What is shared"],[50,"What it decides"],[1556,"What it deliberately does not do:"],[30,"What it does and does not do"],[48,"What it reuses, and the trust warning"],[748,"What it shows"],[410,"What it will not do"],[570,"What it writes: `~/.tmux.conf`, the shared `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` (with the\ncode-server and desktop VS Code User settings symlinked to it), `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`,\n`~/.coder/AGENTS.md`, the rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`, the two terminal helpers in\n`~/.grok/bin`, `~/.grok/notes-setup.sh` plus this clone's `notes.md`, and the\n`~/.grok/bin` line in `~/.bashrc`."],[1686,"What its answers mean here: `REJECTED` beside `env token (GITHUB_TOKEN/GH_TOKEN)` is an\nexpired mint (401 refuses the credential itself, which is what an hour-old injected\ncopy is); `NO GitHub credential in the chain slot` means nothing is connected and\nnothing can be minted; `PUSH-READY` means the credential is fine and you want\nthe push-denied entry\ninstead."],[1040,"What runs here"],[810,"What runs inside a workspace and in the Coder control plane: terminal profiles, the shared `~/.grok` profile, and where models come from."],[1041,"What runs on the box and which door each service answers on"],[826,"What seeds the agent's context"],[1021,"What ships, and which question each one answers: `evo-stack-ops` (pull, status,\nrestart, logs for this stack), `coder-workspace` (workspaces and their GitHub auth),\n`model-routing` (which LiteLLM alias, and how to switch it), `host-linux-ops` (the\nright Linux command, what each flag does, and the blast radius before it runs),\n`host-diagnostics` (read-only triage for a slow, hot, full or failing box), and\n`deploy-from-github` (a named repository to running, with the read-first, pin-the-ref,\nisolate, preflight, health-check, keep-a-rollback order). The last three are what make\nthe terminal door worth opening: they carry the mentoring stance - inspect, explain,\npropose, then act on the yes."],[907,"What the agent is told about all this lives in `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, inlined from\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`. Two sections cover the lanes. **Research\nlanes** makes a lookup part of the normal loop rather than a last resort, with the\nrouting fixed by question type: a library, framework, CLI or setup question goes to\n`context7` (resolve the library id first, then query docs on that id); anything else\non the web goes to the `searxng` `search` tool; a URL already in hand goes to\n`searxng`'s `fetch_content`, because Grok's native `web_fetch` is unavailable here\ntoo - `features.web_fetch` is on, but on a non-xAI endpoint that tool only runs\nthrough `toolset.web_fetch.proxy_endpoint`, and this stack sets none. It also states\nthat native `web_search` is parked so nobody spends a turn rediscovering the 400.\n**Tool output stays out of the parent context** makes that\ntool use delegated work: the child returns a digest - numbered findings, each one\nclaim, one source URL and at most two lines of support, no raw result dumps,\nconfidence stated - and keeps the raw pages to itself."],[965,"What the container mounts, and what \"be the host\" costs"],[1553,"What the fix changed, and how to land it:"],[1238,"What the scaffold repository ships, and the account-wide gaps it closes"],[1231,"What the scaffold ships"],[52,"What the startup script does, in order"],[26,"What the two templates make"],[71,"What the two templates make, and when the `devcontainer` toggle matters"],[535,"What this is not"],[827,"What this is not"],[785,"What to read next"],[1089,"What was measured"],[1732,"What you see is `gh: command not found`, or `fix` refusing with `cannot install the\ncredential helper - gh is not installed in this environment; install gh (apt install gh,\nor the pinned copy the golden image ships) and run this again`. `check` reports the same\nhole honestly: with no gh there is no `gh stored login` slot to read, and a chain with\nnothing else in it prints `NO GitHub credential`."],[1701,"What you see is one of: `remote: Invalid username or token. Password authentication is\nnot supported for Git operations.`, `remote: Repository not found.`, `fatal:\nAuthentication failed for 'https://github.com/...'`, or `fatal: could not read Username\nfor 'https://github.com'`. None of them names the token or its age, and the first is\nwhat a refused token plus a silent askpass looks like, not anything to do with\npasswords."],[1695,"What you see, in that `coder_script`'s log, is its own two lines:"],[1726,"What you see, inside a workspace: `gh api /user`, `gh pr create` or `gh repo view`\nanswers `Bad credentials` (HTTP 401) while the same command on the stack host works, and\n`gh auth status` still insists you are logged in. This is the most frequent GitHub\nfailure here: 19 of 222 past sessions in this workspace hit `Bad credentials`."],[1685,"What you see: `git push` answers `remote: Invalid username or token. Password\nauthentication is not supported for Git operations.`, or `gh auth status` calls the\ntoken invalid. An expired token and a never-connected account read the same from that\nmessage, so ask which one you have before you touch either."],[124,"When a body really does need to grow past the budget, the options are:"],[1085,"When a box answers `NoRoom`"],[1097,"When a pinned box cools or goes unhealthy"],[1195,"When every Spark box is down, `router_settings.fallbacks` degrades `agent` straight to\n`coder`, which is text-only."],[1689,"When nothing can be minted AND the store is empty its line begins `HANDOFF` and names\nthe three clicks below. That line is the boundary: no command run in that workspace can\ndo them."],[599,"When the container itself is the suspect, `bazelisk run //:validate` from inside it is the\nfastest answer, and ../troubleshooting.md covers the stack-side\nfailures it rules in or out."],[9,"Where a hotfix branches from and where it lands first"],[1243,"Where all of this is enforced"],[1104,"Where an alias edit lands"],[33,"Where it runs, and why"],[418,"Where the command may run, and where it refuses to"],[1239,"Where the scaffold is fetched from, and how to pin or replace it"],[68,"Where the token comes from"],[1236,"Where this is enforced"],[1307,"Where to look"],[409,"Where to run it"],[1042,"Where to start depending on what you came to do"],[1735,"Whether you are looking at a missing tool or a missing credential, `check` is what tells\nthem apart: a missing binary shows up as a slot that cannot answer, a missing grant\nshows up as `REFUSED-GRANT`."],[1101,"Which box answered"],[415,"Which files go stale and why `git pull` cannot fix them"],[1241,"Which GitHub steps need which App permission, and what the CLI does when one is refused"],[1309,"Which page answers which operational question"],[1068,"Which page explains which part of the stack"],[693,"Which runtime actually gates a merge, and which one is a convenience"],[685,"Which runtime gates"],[1545,"Which sessions, worst first, with the same `head` cap so a busy store stays readable:"],[617,"While you iterate: the derived test list, and when you still owe one uncached pass"],[609,"While you iterate: which tests, and when you still owe a full pass"],[824,"Whose compaction is whose"],[718,"Why a ledger and not `bash -x`: xtrace traces bats-core itself, wrote 3.4 GB of log\nfor one suite, and leaked trace lines into the stderr bats captures per test, which\nturned output-asserting suites red. The ledger measures the narrower thing the gate\nasks and breaks nothing."],[602,"Why a query, not a grep"],[614,"Why a structural question is a query here, not a grep sweep"],[1733,"Why it blocks the repair rather than just the CLI: the helper `fix` installs is\n`gh auth git-credential` wrapped in `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN ...`, and the\ncredential it points git at is the gh auth store. With no `gh` binary there is no\nenv-proof helper to install and no store to write, so `fix` refuses and says so instead\nof leaving a half-repaired `~/.gitconfig` behind."],[45,"Why the shared bind path is load-bearing"],[53,"Why the stages sit where they do"],[534,"Why this repo's own workspaces stay on `workspace`"],[1105,"Why this shape"],[802,"Wire the control-plane chat agent — print the intended end state, then apply it:"],[1205,"With a pin, it names the pinned box; without one, whichever box `least-busy` picked."],[220,"With it on, per workspace:"],[1211,"With no concurrency cap configured, the proxy neither queued nor shed inside itself,\nand a fully offered box absorbed the overflow in its own scheduler."],[844,"With no tmux (offline first boot) the helper execs a plain login shell, so the\ndefault terminal still opens."],[253,"With the toggle off - or on a repo that carries no `devcontainer.json`, where the build\nhas nothing to build - the editor attaches to the plain workspace agent instead, opened\non the same clone path."],[251,"With the toggle on, `coder_devcontainer.repo` then builds the repo's own\n`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` on the sidecar through the Coder agent\n(`@devcontainers/cli` is installed into the parent workspace container by a\n`run_on_start` module, and the golden image does not ship it)."],[640,"Without `--json` the same labels are interleaved with the report lines, so piping them straight into\nanother command feeds it `ok: tests-for` as an argument. Paths are repo-relative, and a batch costs\none query: when a batch fails, the verb re-runs it per path and names the path the graph does not\nknow instead of hiding every other answer."],[374,"Without the flag the script passes exactly the parameters it used to pass, and the create\nform's **Git repositories (additional)** textarea stays empty."],[4,"Work branches"],[1015,"Working agreement with the assistant: it explains the plan in chat, then waits for the\napproval card in the Chat tab before running anything mutating. If a run looks like it\nis acting without one, stop it and re-read the mode."],[1431,"Workspace agents never reach Authelia either way. The template rewrites the agent URL\nto `http://host.docker.internal:3002`, the plain listener published through\n`CODER_AGENT_TUNNEL_PORT`, so agent traffic stays on the box."],[1449,"Workspace cannot reach LiteLLM -> `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, or `host.docker.internal`"],[1454,"Workspace stuck in *starting* -> startup is blocking"],[1120,"Workspaces and desktops always talk to one base URL,\n`http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`, and pick a model alias. That is the\nhost-gateway door."],[1432,"Workspaces created before public mode existed need a template re-push plus a\nrestart, to pick up the template's `coder_agent_url` variable (default\n`http://host.docker.internal:3002`). That variable is what keeps agents dialing the\nlocal plain listener whatever the access URL says."],[1226,"Workspaces see a stable OpenAI-compatible URL and stable alias names."],[1453,"Workspaces: startup, images, the toolchain inside them"],[1493,"Workspaces: startup, the golden image, Grok Build (`/ez-learn`, MCP), the toolchain"],[287,"Writes the two terminal-profile tmux helpers (see below). 